# Pagination

Page-by-page navigation through a long collection — prev/next plus
a numbered window with ellipses.

<%= embed :pagination, :default %>

## When to use

- Long collections consumed a page at a time, almost always under a
  `table`.

## When not to use

- Collections that fit on two screens — show them all; pagination
  costs a click per screenful.
- Feeds where position doesn't matter — but infinite scroll is an
  app-level choice, not shipped here; default to pagination.
- Stepping through a flow — pagination navigates a collection, not
  a process.

## Usage

`render_pagination` (`Gesso::Components::PaginationHelper`) renders a handed-in
window — **it does no page maths**. The controller (or view) computes
which numbers and gaps to show and hands the window in.

```erb
<%%= render_pagination(
  previous: { path: "?page=2" },
  next_page: { path: "?page=4" },
  pages: [
    { number: 1, path: "?page=1", current: false },
    :gap,
    { number: 3, path: "?page=3", current: true },
    { number: 4, path: "?page=4", current: false },
    :gap,
    { number: 10, path: "?page=10", current: false }
  ]
) %>
```

| Param | Notes |
| ----- | ----- |
| `pages` | array of `{ number:, path:, current: }` hashes and `:gap` symbols (ellipses) |
| `previous` | `{ path: }`, or nil to omit (first page) |
| `next_page` | `{ path: }`, or nil to omit (last page). Named to avoid the `next` keyword |

Current page renders the `outline` button variant at `icon` size with
`aria-current`; others use `ghost`. The previous and next links mark
their chevron `data-icon="inline-start"` / `"inline-end"` so basecoat
adjusts the padding.

## Do and don't

- Do put the page number in the URL (`?page=3`) — shareable,
  reloadable (see *Navigation*: state in the URL).
- Do keep the window small: first, last, current ±1, gaps between —
  the controller decides, consistently across the app.
- Do omit `previous`/`next_page` at the ends (pass nil) rather than
  rendering them disabled (see *Navigation*: omit, don't disable).
- Don't reset filters or sort when the page changes; page links
  carry the full query.
- Don't show pagination for a single page of results.

## Accessibility and content

- A labelled `<nav>` ("Pagination"); prev/next links carry explicit
  labels ("Go to previous page"); the current page is announced via
  `aria-current`.
- Ellipses are visual gap markers, not links; screen readers skip
  to the real pages.
